WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.460
So I only have one picture of myself from the age of 23.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.680
[voiceover] This is the only photographic evidence I have

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.280
of the year I spent in Chicago in an intense and loving, but doomed relationship;

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.100
the year I started and restarted the book that became

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.140
Looking for Alaska;

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.940
and also the year when, to borrow a line from Emily Dickinson,

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.100
"A Plank in Reason, broke,
And I dropped down, and down -"

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.900
You know what, I'm assigning feelings to that picture

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.140
that aren't really in it, because I was quite happy

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.220
when that photograph was taken.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.120
But anyway, I was thinking about it recently

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.700
because, for the past decade or so, my life has been,

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.380
you know, significantly better photographed.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.060
Like, I have hundreds of pictures of myself at 39,

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.660
and, between Sarah and me, we've taken over a thousand

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.440
pictures of our kids this year.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.700
Which is great! But, I feel like we citizens of the internet

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.660
are in general better at creating stuff than we are at

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.820
preserving, curating, and archiving it.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.000
So I wonder how many of our pictures will still be around in 15 years.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Like, it's often posited that when archeologists

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.800
of the distant future look at our period of human history, they'll be like,

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.740
"Abundance increased dramatically in the 19th and 20th centuries,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.460
and then people started building fewer amazing churches

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.160
and instead they began to worship screens."

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.420
And since they'll have no idea what was inside of those screens

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.800
and the data will be corroded,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.400
the people of the future will know about Wikipedia only because

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.560
of, like, the printed books that mentioned it.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.740
Which is an interesting idea, but I have a couple problems with it.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.020
First, uh, it assumes that in the distant future, there will be people.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.160
Secondly, it ignores the many archivists who are working very hard

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.480
to ensure that some slice of digital life is preserved for future historians.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.640
In fact, that's why I started thinking about this in the first place;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.600
An academic library recently got in touch with us

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.020
about preserving some stuff from Brotherhood 2.0 and Nerdfighteria.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.280
Now, of course it would be nice to have more than one picture of myself from when I was 23,

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.880
but we don't want to archive everything forever, right?

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.280
I mean, for one thing, there is no forever,

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.420
but also, like, I'm grateful that I have one picture of my great-grandmother,

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.020
I'm not sure I'd need 17,000 of them.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.800
Also, if we start to feel like life isn't really real unless

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.460
or until it's photographed, we risk undervaluing

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:59.360
all the really important parts of life that aren't very photogenic.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.620
You know, like grief.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.040
But just to be clear, I am not one of those old people

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.280
who thinks it's bad news that young people are taking so many pictures.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.120
These days, photographs aren't just a way of remembering;

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.960
they're a way of having a conversation with yourself and others.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.340
My concern is only that in constantly creating new things,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.640
we risk forgetting about the old things

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.860
that might someday be useful to us.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.360
Like, there were once more photos of me at 23.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.100
For instance, there were some on Friendster, a now-defunct social networking site,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.960
but those pictures died with Friendster.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.940
Now, Facebook and Instagram will likely be around for decades, if not centuries

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.780
and lots of us are backing up our poems and our work and photographs to the cloud.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:39.000
But when I go to the internet archive and go back even a few years to see what Nerdfighteria used to look like,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.680
there are already holes.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.360
I can see what the vlogbrothers channel looked like

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.320
at the end of 2007, that we'd made 237 videos

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.120
and had 9,000 subscribers.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.560
Or I can see what the page looked like in July of 2009

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.440
when we had 90,000 subscribers.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:52.740
But a lot is already gone.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.100
The comment that started the Evil Baby Orphanage is gone.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.360
Lots of the early Nerdfighter artwork is gone.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.740
Sites are shuttered. Links are dead.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.680
Now, I don't want Nerdfighteria to be a backwards-facing community.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.160
And I don't labor under the delusion

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.420
that our little corner of the internet will be significant to scholars,

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.020
but it's significant to me.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.180
And since that library emailed, I've been thinking about

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.620
what to archive and how to archive it.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.300
And so I thought I would ask you, whether you've

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.480
been around for a long time or you're new in town,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.800
when you think of vlogbrothers and Nerdfighteria,  what do you think of?

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.700
What, if anything, should be archived?

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.200
And do you have copies of it, because I might not.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.480
Also, a little piece of advice.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.260
If you're 23, or even if you're not, take a bunch of pictures of yourself this year.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.040
Save them to the cloud, but then also maybe print some of them out.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.960
Just in case the physical things end up being the ones that last.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.780
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:39.360
[click]

